    "title": "Jacksonville leaders and military join for mission readiness - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Abbie Sudbury ,\n7 hours ago\nJACKSONVILLE, N.C. (WNCT) — Military and local government leaders from across eastern North Carolina gathered to reinforce their shared commitment to mission readiness, community partnership, and regional resilience.\nMore than 30 military and local government leaders from across eastern North Carolina gathered March 17 at The Landing, an all-ranks club on Marine Corps Air Station New River, to reinforce their shared commitment to mission readiness, community partnership, and regional resilience.\nCol. Gregory B. Pace, deputy commander, Marine Corps Installations East, Marine Corps Base Camp Lejeune, welcomed elected officials, community leaders, and military partners, emphasizing the unique relationship between the installation and surrounding communities.\n“Approximately 75 percent of our population lives off base,” Pace said. “We are not separate—we are one community, one ecosystem. This is one of the best examples I’ve seen of military communities and local governments all rowing in the same direction.”\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WNCT.",
    "summary": "JACKSONVILLE, N.C. (WNCT) — Military and local government leaders from across eastern North Carolina gathered to reinforce their shared commitment to mission readiness, community partnership, and regional resilience. More than 30 military and local government leaders from across eastern North Carolina gathered March 17 at The Landing, an all-ranks club on Marine Corps Air Station New River, to reinforce their shared commitment to mission readiness, community partnership, and regional resilience. Pace, deputy commander, Marine Corps Installations East, Marine Corps Base Camp Lejeune, welcomed elected officials, community leaders, and military partners, emphasizing the unique relationship between the installation and surrounding communities.",
